### 14. PRESENTATION MODE FOR JUDGES

Add a prominent "Judge Demo Mode" button.

This mode should simplify the interface and present a guided demonstration.

Use the following sequence.

STEP 1 — Introduce the insurance claim.

Show the original claim details and explain the roles of Agents A through E.

STEP 2 — Show the normal workflow.

Run one clean claim through all five agents.

Show successful verification at all four handoffs.

STEP 3 — Generate a new error scenario.

Randomly choose an error, such as data loss, manipulation, or an incorrect calculation.

Show the generated test case and its expected outcome.

STEP 4 — Run the simulation.

Animate the data passing from Agent A to Agent B, then B to C, C to D, and D to E.

STEP 5 — Reveal the error.

Stop at the affected checkpoint.

Highlight the original and received values.

Display the error category, severity, and explanation.

STEP 6 — Show TrustMesh intervention.

Explain how the framework detected the discrepancy and whether it blocked, retried, or routed the transaction for human review.

STEP 7 — Inspect the audit trail.

Display the exact event records associated with the error.

STEP 8 — Demonstrate recovery.

If the error is recoverable, retry the transfer using the original verified data or an authorized corrected payload.

Show the recovery result without deleting the original failure event.

STEP 9 — Show the final outcome.

Explain whether the claim was approved, rejected, blocked, or sent for human review.

STEP 10 — Show the impact.

Summarize what would have happened without TrustMesh and how verification reduced the risk of an incorrect decision.

Make the entire demonstration possible through buttons, without requiring the presenter to edit code or type commands.

